WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Can the gate from chapter one fail while every gatekeeper remains present?

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.240
The purple inhibitory neuron, its ending, and the red target all remain.

3
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.660
This time, the change occurs inside the target neuron.

4
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:23.233
An inhibitory synapse opens channels permeable to chloride and bicarbonate.

5
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:29.373
Its current depends on the difference between membrane potential and anion reversal

6
00:00:29.373 --> 00:00:36.273
potential. Teal KCC2 helps keep intracellular chloride low in these spinal neurons.

7
00:00:37.417 --> 00:00:42.077
In adult male rats, nerve injury reduced dorsal-horn KCC2.

8
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:48.857
In acute lamina I slices, anion reversal shifted in the depolarizing direction.

9
00:00:50.157 --> 00:00:54.177
The same synaptic conductance now produced current with a different sign.

10
00:00:55.267 --> 00:01:00.887
Use one computed case at a membrane potential of minus −65 mV.

11
00:01:02.107 --> 00:01:06.567
Reversal at minus −75 gives plus +10 mV of driving force.

12
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:14.287
Move reversal to minus −50 and driving force becomes minus −15 Conductance stayed

13
00:01:14.287 --> 00:01:17.207
fixed. Current direction changed.

14
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.770
Current reversal is not the whole claim.

15
00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.410
An open anion conductance can still shunt other current.

16
00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:32.130
A depolarizing response becomes excitatory only if its net effect raises firing.

17
00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:36.630
The rat experiments measured increased excitability too.

18
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.910
Now predict the diagnostic pattern.

19
00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.330
Cell counts remain stable after injury.

20
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:50.230
Which added measurement can reveal a changed postsynaptic effect: more counting, an

21
00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:55.070
evoked output recording, or anion reversal measured without replacing chloride?

22
00:02:01.183 --> 00:02:05.383
Measure anion reversal with a gramicidin perforated patch.

23
00:02:06.563 --> 00:02:10.043
It gives electrical access while preserving the cell's chloride.

24
00:02:11.563 --> 00:02:12.943
Stable counts test survival.

25
00:02:14.243 --> 00:02:16.383
Evoked inhibitory events test output.

26
00:02:17.823 --> 00:02:20.703
Reversal tests the target's response to that output.

27
00:02:21.783 --> 00:02:24.763
The rat studies also traced the signal upstream.

28
00:02:26.163 --> 00:02:29.483
ATP-stimulated microglia released BDNF.

29
00:02:30.763 --> 00:02:35.163
Gold BDNF blocks then reached TrkB on the red target.

30
00:02:36.217 --> 00:02:40.617
Gold BDNF then moved the reversal marker in the target.

31
00:02:41.957 --> 00:02:46.237
These rat transfer experiments did not measure KCC2 afterwards.

32
00:02:47.597 --> 00:02:50.117
The purple synapse remained present throughout the shift.

33
00:02:51.217 --> 00:02:53.037
Compare three pure patterns.

34
00:02:54.477 --> 00:02:56.657
Cell death lowers the count and evoked events.

35
00:02:58.217 --> 00:03:01.217
Lost output leaves counts stable but reduces events.

36
00:03:02.477 --> 00:03:07.517
A changed postsynaptic effect leaves counts stable and events present while reversal

37
00:03:07.517 --> 00:03:11.017
moves. Real mechanisms can coexist.

38
00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:15.087
This route was established in adult male rats.

39
00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:20.007
It is not automatically obligatory across species or sexes.

40
00:03:21.387 --> 00:03:23.267
Keep the experimental rule instead.

41
00:03:24.687 --> 00:03:28.507
Count cells, test output, then measure reversal in the target neuron.
